msgid ""
"The share of your household consumption met without drawing from the "
"grid: 1 − grid import ÷ consumption. The left figure is the baseline — "
"what this same period would have looked like without a battery — and the "
"right figure is the simulated result with the battery you configured. "
"Both come from the simulation, so the two are a like-for-like comparison."
" This is why the left figure can differ by a point or two from the self-"
"sufficiency shown for your household higher up the page: that one is "
"measured straight from your meter. Your meter records importing and "
"exporting at separate moments within the same hour, whereas the "
"simulation works in whole intervals, so those partly cancel out and it "
"reproduces slightly less grid import. Comparing your measured figure "
"against a simulated one would overstate what the battery adds."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The share of your household consumption met without drawing from the "
"grid: self-sufficiency = 1 − grid import ÷ consumption. 0% means every "
"kWh came from the grid; higher means more was covered by your own solar "
"(and existing battery). It uses the reconstructed consumption above."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The share of your solar production you used yourself rather than "
"exporting: self-consumption = 1 − grid export ÷ solar produced, measured "
"over the period your solar data covers. 100% means none of your "
"production was exported; lower means more went back to the grid."
